{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pl-07-equal-gain-loss-asymmetry",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Equal Percentage Gain and Loss Cases",
    "description": "Understand why equal percentage gains and losses do not automatically cancel, and select the right comparison base.",
    "teachingApproach": "Develop the commercial relationship step by step, explain why each rate uses its base, show intermediate amounts, check signs and units, then practice transfer with misconception-specific retries.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 12 “Profit and Loss,” printed pages 374–425. Approved scope: Analyze two transactions with equal percentage gain and loss, explaining the base difference and when the standard loss formula applies. The familiar x% gain and x% loss produce a net loss only when the two compared starting costs are equal (or when the problem specifies equal selling prices and the appropriate derived costs); do not apply the shortcut without its conditions. Compare equal CP, equal SP, and unequal transactions using amounts, and verify the whole result from total CP and SP. Use solved example 27 and relevant objective problems."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Equal Percentage Gain and Loss Cases: Understand why equal percentage gains and losses do not automatically cancel, and select the right comparison base. We establish the model, explain each step, and practice with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The phrase “20% gain and 20% loss” is incomplete until we know whether the two items had equal costs or equal selling prices. Percentages describe a ratio, and the base determines the currency amount.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the two starting costs are equal, a 20% gain on one and 20% loss on the other have equal currency amounts. The combined result is break-even, assuming both initial costs are the same.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the two items sell for the same amount, their CPs are not equal. For a common SP S, CP at x% gain is S/(1+x/100), while CP at x% loss is S/(1−x/100).",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For equal SP and rates ±x%, the total loss rate is x²/100 percent. At 20%, the two costs total 2.0833S while receipts total 2S, giving a 4% loss on total cost.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The formula works only for equal selling prices and equal percentage magnitudes. A different pair of prices, costs, or rates requires direct calculation.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A common intuition error is “plus 20 and minus 20 cancel.” The second percentage may use a different denominator, so equal percentages need not represent equal money.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the costs differ, find each gain/loss in currency. Example: +20% on ₹500 is +₹100; −20% on ₹1,000 is −₹200; combined loss is ₹100 on ₹1,500, or 6⅔%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For equal SP but unequal rates, recover each cost separately. If one item earns 25% and another loses 10%, then CPs are S/1.25 and S/0.9.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When only equal cost is stated, compute the common CP and apply each signed rate. Equal CP makes the rate average valid; unequal CP makes it a weighted average.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "“Two similar items” in a shortcut problem usually means equal sale prices when that is explicitly stated. Never assume “similar” silently means equal costs if the actual question gives a different condition.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Check the combined ledger: total SP is the sum received; total CP is the sum invested. The sign of SP−CP settles gain or loss without relying on a mnemonic.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "If the two SPs differ by a known amount, a gain on one and loss on another may still be determined from CP information. Use the actual figures; the equal-SP shortcut no longer applies.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "For equal SP and +x/−x, the loss amount increases quickly with x because it depends on x². At 10%, the loss rate is 1%; at 30%, it is 9%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Boundary check: at x=0 there is no gain or loss. At x=100%, an item sold at a 100% loss has zero receipt and the equal-SP setup is impossible for a positive SP.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "State the condition beside any shortcut in your work, then verify with CP and SP totals on a simple base such as ₹100 per sale.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
